\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces  Best observed mean predictive performance in each simulation scenario among Poisson and negative-binomial models. For IPCW IBS and cure RMSE, smaller values are better, whereas for cure AUC, larger values are better. Parenthesized values are Monte Carlo standard errors. The displayed optimum is a descriptive comparison and is not a claim of superiority when paired uncertainty includes zero. GG, Spl, P, and NB denote generalized-gamma kernel machine, spline, Poisson, and negative binomial, respectively. For GG-KM fits, the selected kernel is shown in parentheses. }}{11}{table.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:simulation-best}{{1}{11}{Best observed mean predictive performance in each simulation scenario among Poisson and negative-binomial models. For IPCW IBS and cure RMSE, smaller values are better, whereas for cure AUC, larger values are better. Parenthesized values are Monte Carlo standard errors. The displayed optimum is a descriptive comparison and is not a claim of superiority when paired uncertainty includes zero. GG, Spl, P, and NB denote generalized-gamma kernel machine, spline, Poisson, and negative binomial, respectively. For GG-KM fits, the selected kernel is shown in parentheses}{table.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces  Paired regret and descriptive rank summaries over the 18 primary simulation scenarios for the Poisson and negative-binomial models. The comparison is restricted to spline models and GG-KM models using the RBF kernel. Within each scenario and metric, the reference is the retained method with the best finite mean. For IPCW integrated Brier score and cure RMSE, regret is the method value minus the reference value; for cure AUC, regret is the reference value minus the method value, so that smaller regret is better for all metrics. Regret uses only replication pairs for which both methods are finite. The worst regret identifies its scenario and reports a 95\% Monte Carlo half-width; mean regret weights scenarios equally and propagates their Monte Carlo standard errors. Mean rank is based on scenario means and is descriptive. }}{11}{table.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:simulation-regret}{{2}{11}{Paired regret and descriptive rank summaries over the 18 primary simulation scenarios for the Poisson and negative-binomial models. The comparison is restricted to spline models and GG-KM models using the RBF kernel. Within each scenario and metric, the reference is the retained method with the best finite mean. For IPCW integrated Brier score and cure RMSE, regret is the method value minus the reference value; for cure AUC, regret is the reference value minus the method value, so that smaller regret is better for all metrics. Regret uses only replication pairs for which both methods are finite. The worst regret identifies its scenario and reports a 95\% Monte Carlo half-width; mean regret weights scenarios equally and propagates their Monte Carlo standard errors. Mean rank is based on scenario means and is descriptive}{table.caption.6}{}}
