\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Count-matched paired predictive differences of the RBF GG-KM against the linear-kernel GG-KM, additive-spline, and polynomial-kernel GG-KM comparators. Columns correspond to the linear, quadratic, and periodic cure surfaces, while rows show IPCW integrated Brier score, cure root mean squared error, and cure AUC differences. Each contrast is defined as the RBF GG-KM value minus the corresponding comparator value, with Poisson and negative-binomial fits compared separately. Error bars span $\pm 1.96$ paired Monte Carlo standard errors, and a horizontal line marks zero. Negative differences favor the RBF GG-KM for integrated Brier score and cure root mean squared error, whereas positive differences favor it for cure AUC.}}{12}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:simulation-paired-differences}{{3}{12}{Count-matched paired predictive differences of the RBF GG-KM against the linear-kernel GG-KM, additive-spline, and polynomial-kernel GG-KM comparators. Columns correspond to the linear, quadratic, and periodic cure surfaces, while rows show IPCW integrated Brier score, cure root mean squared error, and cure AUC differences. Each contrast is defined as the RBF GG-KM value minus the corresponding comparator value, with Poisson and negative-binomial fits compared separately. Error bars span $\pm 1.96$ paired Monte Carlo standard errors, and a horizontal line marks zero. Negative differences favor the RBF GG-KM for integrated Brier score and cure root mean squared error, whereas positive differences favor it for cure AUC}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Pointwise accuracy of the three primary cure surfaces at $n=600$ under Poisson generation. Rows show the linear, quadratic and periodic generating surfaces; columns show the linear-kernel GG-KM, additive-spline, polynomial-kernel GG-KM and radial-basis-function (RBF) GG-KM predictors. The filled field is the root mean squared error of the predicted cure probability at each of the $41\times 41$ grid points, taken across all valid replications, on one scale shared by every panel and truncated above 0.30. Solid labelled lines are the true cure levels $0.25$, $0.50$ and $0.60$; the dashed line is the mean estimated $0.50$ level and is annotated as absent when the fitted range does not contain it. The value printed in each panel is the mean of the displayed field. Failed fits remain in $n_{\mathrm  {total}}$ and reduce the pointwise $n_{\mathrm  {valid}}$ rather than disappearing.}}{13}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cure-surface-recovery}{{4}{13}{Pointwise accuracy of the three primary cure surfaces at $n=600$ under Poisson generation. Rows show the linear, quadratic and periodic generating surfaces; columns show the linear-kernel GG-KM, additive-spline, polynomial-kernel GG-KM and radial-basis-function (RBF) GG-KM predictors. The filled field is the root mean squared error of the predicted cure probability at each of the $41\times 41$ grid points, taken across all valid replications, on one scale shared by every panel and truncated above 0.30. Solid labelled lines are the true cure levels $0.25$, $0.50$ and $0.60$; the dashed line is the mean estimated $0.50$ level and is annotated as absent when the fitted range does not contain it. The value printed in each panel is the mean of the displayed field. Failed fits remain in $n_{\mathrm {total}}$ and reduce the pointwise $n_{\mathrm {valid}}$ rather than disappearing}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces  Best observed mean predictive performance in each high-dimensional simulation scenario. For IPCW IBS and cure RMSE, smaller values are better, whereas for cure AUC, larger values are better. Parenthesized values are Monte Carlo standard errors. The comparison includes Poisson and negative-binomial GG-KM models, Poisson and negative-binomial spline models, the mixture-cure model, and the promotion-time model. Int and Rad denote Interaction and Radial surfaces, respectively. GG, Spl, P, and NB denote generalized-gamma kernel machine, spline, Poisson, and negative binomial, respectively. For GG-KM fits, the selected kernel is shown in parentheses. }}{13}{table.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:highdim-best}{{3}{13}{Best observed mean predictive performance in each high-dimensional simulation scenario. For IPCW IBS and cure RMSE, smaller values are better, whereas for cure AUC, larger values are better. Parenthesized values are Monte Carlo standard errors. The comparison includes Poisson and negative-binomial GG-KM models, Poisson and negative-binomial spline models, the mixture-cure model, and the promotion-time model. Int and Rad denote Interaction and Radial surfaces, respectively. GG, Spl, P, and NB denote generalized-gamma kernel machine, spline, Poisson, and negative binomial, respectively. For GG-KM fits, the selected kernel is shown in parentheses}{table.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Paired predictive differences of the radial-basis GG-KM predictor against the count-matched GG-KM linear-kernel, spline, and GG-KM polynomial-kernel comparators on the interaction and radial cure surfaces. The top row shows paired mean IPCW integrated Brier score differences and the bottom row paired mean cure root mean squared error differences. Each plotted contrast is defined as the RBF GG-KM value minus the corresponding comparator value, so negative differences favor the RBF GG-KM and positive differences favor the comparator. Solid lines with filled markers denote Poisson fits, whereas dashed lines with open markers denote negative-binomial fits. Each panel displays the six generating-law and sample-size cells, with error bars spanning $\pm 1.96$ paired Monte Carlo standard errors.}}{14}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:highdim-paired-differences}{{5}{14}{Paired predictive differences of the radial-basis GG-KM predictor against the count-matched GG-KM linear-kernel, spline, and GG-KM polynomial-kernel comparators on the interaction and radial cure surfaces. The top row shows paired mean IPCW integrated Brier score differences and the bottom row paired mean cure root mean squared error differences. Each plotted contrast is defined as the RBF GG-KM value minus the corresponding comparator value, so negative differences favor the RBF GG-KM and positive differences favor the comparator. Solid lines with filled markers denote Poisson fits, whereas dashed lines with open markers denote negative-binomial fits. Each panel displays the six generating-law and sample-size cells, with error bars spanning $\pm 1.96$ paired Monte Carlo standard errors}{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Predictive performance in 50 paired repeated holdouts of the melanoma cohort. Values for the IPCW integrated Brier score (IBS), cure-discrimination area under the curve ($\mathrm  {AUC}_{\mathrm  {cure}}$), and mean predicted cure fraction are means across successful splits, with split standard deviations in parentheses. Convergence is reported as the number of converged fits among successful fits. Runtime is the mean with the maximum in parentheses. }}{15}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:melanoma-performance}{{4}{15}{Predictive performance in 50 paired repeated holdouts of the melanoma cohort. Values for the IPCW integrated Brier score (IBS), cure-discrimination area under the curve ($\mathrm {AUC}_{\mathrm {cure}}$), and mean predicted cure fraction are means across successful splits, with split standard deviations in parentheses. Convergence is reported as the number of converged fits among successful fits. Runtime is the mean with the maximum in parentheses}{table.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Predictive assessment in 50 paired repeated holdouts of the melanoma cohort. Panel A compares the full-cohort Kaplan--Meier curve with split-averaged out-of-sample survival predictions over the common supported horizon. Panel B shows split-level IPCW integrated Brier scores, with each point representing one outer split and lower values indicating better prediction. Line type, marker or box encoding, and labels distinguish all eight methods without relying on color alone. Panel A shows mean curves without an uncertainty band; the dependence caveat for repeated holdouts is stated in the text.}}{15}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:melanoma-performance}{{6}{15}{Predictive assessment in 50 paired repeated holdouts of the melanoma cohort. Panel A compares the full-cohort Kaplan--Meier curve with split-averaged out-of-sample survival predictions over the common supported horizon. Panel B shows split-level IPCW integrated Brier scores, with each point representing one outer split and lower values indicating better prediction. Line type, marker or box encoding, and labels distinguish all eight methods without relying on color alone. Panel A shows mean curves without an uncertainty band; the dependence caveat for repeated holdouts is stated in the text}{figure.caption.10}{}}
